## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to arbitrary bit widths or detect overflow across the 128-bit boundary?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, alternating patterns, and patterns crossing the boundary?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

