- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with bit-slicing and fixed-mask patterns on multi-limb integers, not just 64-bit tricks. It also checks understanding of dividing work across 128-bit by treating halves independently. 

## Key Concepts
- Fixed masks for pairwise swaps (even/odd bits)
- 128-bit represented as two 64-bit lanes
- Branchless, constant-time bit permutation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t A = 0x5555555555555555ULL; // even bits
  const uint64_t B = 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL; // odd bits
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & A) << 1) | ((x.lo & B) >> 1);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & A) << 1) | ((x.hi & B) >> 1);
  return r;
}
```

